  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Wednesday) \nIssued at 921 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThe primary talking point for the long term is the returning rain\nchances through the week. Late this weekend, an upper level trough is\nforecast to drop southeastward into the Tennessee Valley and\nAppalachian region. A subsequent sfc low will shift into the\nsoutheast U.S., bringing an accompanying cold front eastward into our\ncoverage area. In response, widespread showers/thunderstorms are\nforecast through the area, with higher rain chances remaining along\nand east of I-65 (though this depends on exact frontal placement and\nmay change between now and then). Recent ensembles have come into\nbetter agreement about the placement of the aforementioned trough and\ntherefore has increased our confidence in at least medium rain\nchances occurring through the weekend into early next week. As the\nupper level trough shifts northeastward, lingering rain chances\ncontinue into mid week as low pressure continues to reside over the\narea. At the tail end of the long term, an upper level ridge and\nsubsequent high pressure begin to work eastward into the lower\nMississippi Valley which should begin to dry out the area along with\nincreasing temperatures yet again. Highs throughout the long term\nperiod are forecast to warm into the 80s with overnight lows in the\nupper 60s-lower 70s.",
